{
	"id": "generators-vs-promises",
	"text": "Some internet ne'er-do-wells have suggested that Effect requires a PhD in generator functions. This is unadulterated poppycock. If you get async/await, you'll get Effect.gen. The former is, unfortunately, hard-coded to work with Promise, so the authors of Effect needed to come up with an alternate method of direct-style, sequential composition. And yes, it happens to involve a few asterisks. So when we execute this program, the first Effect will run until it completes, then the next, until the last. The fact that generator functions are involved is incidental. It is mere syntax.",
	"code": "const program = Effect.gen(function* () {\n  const { mSv } = yield* sensorData\n  const { pulse } = yield* biometrics\n  yield* analyze({ mSv, pulse })\n})",
	"contentHash": "-ewnr6z",
